<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Install:  cp this to ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ and
            launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.talaria.daemon.plist

  Two things launchd is fussy about, both deliberate here:

  - ProgramArguments points at an app bundle's executable rather than straight
    at node. System Settings › Login Items names a background item after the app
    responsible for it, resolved through the code signature — with no bundle to
    be responsible, the item is listed as "Node", which is accurate and useless.
    The bundle also gives CoreSpotlight (Phase 2) a bundle id it can resolve;
    without one it refuses to index, at index time rather than at call time.

    The bundle's executable picks the absolute node itself. There are three node
    installs on this Mac and PATH decides between them, and PATH is exactly what
    a LaunchAgent does not reliably get.
  - KeepAlive restarts a crash and not a clean exit, which is not the same as
    unconditional and had to stop being unconditional.

    A GUI app bundle spawned straight by launchd does not stay the process
    launchd spawned. macOS re-registers it through LaunchServices, the original
    exits 0, and with an unconditional KeepAlive launchd starts another one
    twenty seconds later — which does the same thing. It ran 689 times in two
    days, 551 of them in a single morning, each leaving one line in the log
    saying it had stepped aside because an instance was already running. Nothing
    was broken and nothing said so; it simply spawned a process every twenty
    seconds forever.

    SuccessfulExit: false is the distinction that was missing. A clean exit is
    something having finished — the hand-off above, or a person quitting the app
    — and starting it again is overruling them.

    Be clear about what this job therefore is, because it is easy to read it as
    more: it *starts* Talaria at login and it does not supervise it. The process
    launchd spawns hands off and exits within a second, so from then on launchd
    has nothing to watch and a crash does not bring the app back. That was
    equally true under the unconditional setting — the retries were not
    supervision, they were the hand-off being attempted again every twenty
    seconds — so nothing was given up here. Real supervision would mean pointing
    the job at node and the daemon script directly, which is the trade the
    ProgramArguments note above declines for the sake of Login Items and
    Spotlight having a bundle to name.

    The cost is that a genuinely bad config respawns forever. ThrottleInterval
    holds that to once a minute rather than once every ten seconds, so the log
    stays readable and the message stays findable. Recovery after a fix is a
    minute at most.

    Because this restarts on a clean exit too, `kill` won't stop it. To actually
    stop it: launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/dev.talaria.daemon

  Restart it after a code change with:
    launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/dev.talaria.daemon
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>dev.talaria.daemon</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/Users/jpmoore/Library/Application Support/Talaria/Talaria.app/Contents/MacOS/Talaria</string>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
    <false/>
  </dict>

  <!--
    Seconds between respawns. The default of 10 turns a bad config into six
    copies of the same error a minute; 60 made every restart during development
    a minute's wait, and a `kickstart` inside the window looks exactly like the
    app failing to start. Twenty is enough to keep a loop readable without that.
  -->
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>20</integer>

  <!--
    Standard, not Background. Background is a claim about a job that never
    touches the interface, and this one owns a menu bar item and a window — a
    job declaring otherwise can be started and then left sleeping before AppKit
    ever hands it applicationDidFinishLaunching, which looks exactly like a hang
    with an empty log.
  -->
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Standard</string>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/jpmoore/Library/Logs/talaria.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/jpmoore/Library/Logs/talaria.log</string>

  <!--
    No WorkingDirectory on purpose. It used to point into ~/Documents, which is
    TCC-protected: a LaunchAgent has no way to ask for that permission and no
    window to ask in, so launchd chdir-ing there before exec can stall the job
    before it runs a line of its own code. The app resolves every path it needs
    absolutely and sets its own working directory for the daemon it spawns.
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
